tions and compromised authentic existence (by mimicing its outward appearance). The happiest laughter is that whose source is a child. For the child must grow up, and 1 know that the insufficiency which it te veals, at which 1 laugb, will be followed by the sufficiency of the adult (time is provided for that). The child is the opportunity to leam without deep anxiety-over an abyss of insufficiency. But just as the child grows up, so does laughter. In its innocent form, it takes place in the same sense as does the constitution of society: laugb ter guarantees it, reinforces it-(the constitution is the throwing of weak forms off towards the periphery): laughter puts those whom it assembles into unanimous convulsions. But laugbter does not reach metely the peripheral region� of existence-it does not have as its object only fools or children (those who became empty or who still are empty); by a necessary reversai, it returns from the child to the father, from the pe riphery to the center, each time that the father or the center betray, in their turn, their insufficiency. (In both cases, we laugh moreover at an identical situation: the unjustified ptetense of suf6ciency.) The necessity for reversaI is so important that it had, at one time, its consecration: there is no constitution of society which does not have, on the other hand, the challenging of its foundations; rituals show it: the saturnalias or festivals of madmen reversed the roles. [And the profund;ty from whence descended blindly the feeling which determ;ned the rituals-the numerous, intimate links between the themes of the carnival and the putting ta death of kings indicate this sufficiently enough.}

If 1 now compare the constitution of society to a pyramid, it appears as a domination on the part of the center, of the summit (this is a rough, even difficult schema). The summit incessandy throws the foundation off into insignificance and, in this sense, waves of laughter traverse the pyra mid by gradually contesting the pretense of sufficiency of the beings placed at a lower level. But the first pattern of these waves issued from the summit flows back and the second pattern traverses the pyramid from the bottom to the top: the flowing back this rime contests the sufficiency of beings placed at a higher level. This contestation, on the other hand, right up to the last instant, preserves the summit: it cannot fail, however, to reach it. In truth, being, without number, is in a certain sense suffocated by a reverberating convulsion: laughter, in particular, suffocates no one, but if 1 envisage the spasm of multitudes (whom one never takes in with a single glance) the flowing back-as 1 have said cannot fail to reach the summit. And if it reaches it? This is the agony of God in black night.

